  <h2 id="the-problem">The Problem: Three Fears Every Beginner Has (and Pricing Pages Make Worse)</h2>
  <p>
    Spend one evening in digital-product communities and the same three posts repeat:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>1. "The fee was supposed to be 10%. Why did my $10 ebook pay me $7.91?"</strong> Because the headline percentage is never the whole cut. Fixed per-transaction fees and card processing stack on top, and they punish cheap products hardest: on Gumroad a $10 sale loses $1.00 (10%) + $0.50 (fixed fee) + $0.29 + $0.30 (card processing) — roughly a fifth of the price. Subscription platforms hide the same cost differently: the fee is 0%, but the $29–$49 monthly rent is due in months when you sell nothing. There is no free option here; there is only the choice of paying per sale or paying per month.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>2. "A buyer in Germany just bought my template. Do I owe VAT somewhere?"</strong> This is the question that separates the four tools more than any feature list. On a merchant-of-record platform (Gumroad since January 2025; Lemon Squeezy by design), the platform is legally the seller, collects and remits sales tax and VAT worldwide, and the fear evaporates. On Podia and Stan Store, you are the seller: the platform calculates and collects tax only in jurisdictions you register in, and filing and remitting is your job. Beginners consistently underestimate this difference because pricing pages advertise fees in bold and tax responsibility in footnotes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>3. "I made my first sales. When do I actually see the money — and can the platform just keep it?"</strong> Every platform here has a gate between "sold" and "paid": Gumroad reviews every new account before the first payout (typically 3–4 sales and a balance over $100, one to three weeks) and holds a $100 minimum; Stan makes you cash out manually from $10; Podia sends money straight to your own Stripe, which is exactly why it has no gate of its own. Layered on top is the loudest complaint pattern in this category: accounts frozen or put "under review" with automated explanations. The gate you can read in advance is fine; the one that surprises you is not.
  </p>

