  <h2 id="pricing-tool-by-tool">Pricing, Tool by Tool — With the Real Limits</h2>

  <h3><a href={gumroadUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gumroad</a> — free to start, expensive per sale</h3>
  <p>
    Gumroad is the only tool here with <strong>no monthly fee and no plans at all</strong>: you pay <strong>10% + $0.50 per direct sale, plus card processing (2.9% + $0.30)</strong> — an all-in cut of about 12.9% + $0.80. On a $25 product that is $4.03 gone; on a $10 product it is $2.09, over a fifth of the price. Sales that come through Gumroad's Discover marketplace cost a flat <strong>30%</strong> (processing included). One genuine improvement: once your paid sales pass <strong>$20,000 in a calendar month</strong>, direct-sale fees drop to 5% + $0.50 for the rest of that month. Since January 1, 2025, Gumroad is the <strong>merchant of record for all sales</strong> — it collects and remits sales tax and VAT worldwide, and you manage no tax settings at all. You can sell downloads, ebooks, courses, memberships and bundles, with files up to 20 GB on paid products.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: payouts run on a schedule you choose (daily/weekly/monthly/quarterly), but there is a <strong>$100 minimum balance</strong>, a minimum 7-day hold, and <strong>every new account is reviewed before its first payout</strong> — Gumroad's own help page says the review typically means 3–4 sales with a balance over $100 and can take one to three weeks. PayPal payouts carry a 2% fee. And the complaint pattern is real: in a counted sample of Gumroad's recent Trustpilot reviews, the dominant themes were suspended accounts, held or skipped payouts, and confusion over the $100 minimum (see production notes). The fees are published and predictable; the review process is the risk you are actually taking.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={stanStoreUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stan Store</a> — 0% fees, $29 rent</h3>
  <p>
    Stan is a link-in-bio storefront built for social traffic: <strong>Creator is $29/mo ($300/yr)</strong> and includes unlimited digital products, a course builder, coaching-call bookings, memberships and community. <strong>Stan takes 0% of your sales</strong> — you pay Stripe's standard 2.9% + $0.30 processing, plus a small <strong>0.25% + $0.25 fee each time you cash out</strong> (minimum $1 per cash-out). The maths flips Gumroad's: Stan is the more expensive option until roughly <strong>$250/mo in sales</strong>, and the cheaper one above it. What the $29 plan does not include is the growth machinery: funnels, upsells, order bumps, discount codes, pixel tracking and actual email sending (broadcasts and flows) all sit on <strong>Creator Pro at $99/mo</strong> — the base plan collects email addresses but cannot send to them. Your own affiliate program (letting customers resell your products) is also Pro-only.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: <strong>you are the seller of record</strong>. Stan's tax feature works through Stripe Tax and only collects in regions where you have registered; exporting the tax report and filing is your job — the opposite of Gumroad's hands-off model, and the thing social-first beginners least expect. Payouts are manual, full-balance cash-outs from a <strong>$10 minimum</strong>; a first sale typically takes 3–7 business days to become cash-out-able. There is no free plan, only a 14-day trial — the rent starts before the revenue does.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={podiaUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Podia</a> — the course business in a box, priced like it</h3>
  <p>
    Podia is the most complete platform here: courses, digital downloads, coaching, community, a website with a custom domain, and email marketing in every plan. <strong>Mover is $49/mo ($42/mo billed yearly)</strong> with a <strong>5% transaction fee</strong>; <strong>Shaker is $99/mo ($84/mo yearly)</strong> with <strong>0% transaction fees</strong>. Card processing (2.9% + $0.30, via your own Stripe or PayPal) is on top of both. Because the money flows through <strong>your own</strong> payment accounts, Podia never holds your revenue — there is no Podia payout threshold or review gate, which is a real structural advantage over Gumroad. The plan caps are the trade: Mover allows 50 products and includes 100 email subscribers (more cost extra), and <strong>PayPal, upsells, Zapier and running your own affiliate program are all gated to Shaker</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: Podia is <strong>not</strong> your merchant of record. It automatically collects sales tax at checkout for jurisdictions <strong>you</strong> register in, across 230 jurisdictions — but registering, filing and remitting stay with you. Podia also has a recent history of charging more for less: the free plan was removed in October 2024, prices rose again in June 2026 (Mover $39 → $49), and features that were once standard keep moving up-tier. At beginner volumes Mover is the most expensive option in this comparison (see the year-one table); it starts winning when your catalogue — not your fee percentage — is the business.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={lemonSqueezyUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lemon Squeezy</a> — the best model in the category, closing down</h3>
  <p>
    On paper, Lemon Squeezy wins this comparison and it is not close: <strong>5% + 50¢ per transaction with payment processing included</strong>, no monthly fee, a true <strong>merchant of record</strong> that takes on tax calculation and remittance liability globally, built-in license keys, and payouts twice a month from a $50 minimum. It is the platform a thousand comparison articles (including, until now, drafts like this one) recommend to beginners.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>It is also closed.</strong> Lemon Squeezy's own homepage and documentation now state that it is <strong>no longer accepting new sign-ups and will stop processing transactions on March 31, 2027</strong>. If you are reading this as a beginner choosing a first platform, Lemon Squeezy is simply not available to you — and any review that still ranks it #1 without that headline is out of date. Existing sellers have a migration window, not a future: the docs point them toward Stripe Managed Payments. We score it 2.5/5 for a beginner in November 2026 — the score is for the closure, not the product, which on fees and tax handling alone would have topped this list.
  </p>

  <h3>The free all-in-one alternative: <a href={systemeAffiliateUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow">Systeme.io</a></h3>
  <p>
    If your plan is "a product <strong>and</strong> an email list <strong>and</strong> a funnel <strong>and</strong> maybe a course", none of the four above bundles that — you would be stacking a store on top of an email tool on top of a funnel tool. Systeme.io's free plan covers selling with <strong>0% platform transaction fees</strong>, plus funnels, one online course and email for up to 2,000 contacts in one dashboard. Its storefront is plainer than Stan's and its course features thinner than Podia's — that trade is the whole story. Full breakdown in our <a href="/blog/systeme-io-review">Systeme.io review</a>, and once the sales start, our <a href="/blog/best-email-marketing-for-beginners">beginner email marketing comparison</a> covers the list-building half of the stack.
  </p>

