  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq-list">
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which of these is actually free to start?</h3>
      <p>
        Only Gumroad, in the sense that matters: no monthly fee, pay per sale. Stan and Podia offer 14-day trials and then charge monthly. Lemon Squeezy was free to start — sign-ups are now closed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Who handles my sales tax and VAT?</h3>
      <p>
        Gumroad and Lemon Squeezy act as merchant of record and collect and remit sales tax/VAT worldwide. On Podia and Stan Store you are the seller: the platform collects tax only in jurisdictions you register in, and filing and remitting are your responsibility. For a beginner selling internationally, this difference is bigger than any fee gap in this article.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why do so many articles still recommend Lemon Squeezy?</h3>
      <p>
        Because until this month it deserved it — and because comparison content ages badly. Lemon Squeezy announced it is no longer accepting new sign-ups and will stop processing payments on March 31, 2027. Check the date on any review that ranks it first.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>When do I actually get paid?</h3>
      <p>
        Gumroad: after a $100 minimum, a 7-day hold and a one-time new-account review (typically 3–4 sales, one to three weeks), then on your chosen schedule. Stan: manual cash-out from $10, typically 2–7 business days after a sale becomes available. Podia: immediately into your own Stripe/PayPal, on the processor's normal timing — no Podia gate at all.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I switch later without losing my customers?</h3>
      <p>
        Your customer list and product files export from all four. What does not move is the plumbing: product pages, course structure, memberships and affiliate setups must be rebuilt. That rebuild cost — plus, on Gumroad and Stan, un-exportable storefront polish — is why the first choice deserves an hour of fee maths rather than a coin flip.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bottom-line">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    For a beginner, the decision is not "which platform is best" but "which cost shape fits the sales you actually have." Gumroad charges you only when you sell and quietly removes the tax problem — at the highest per-sale price here. Stan charges you every month and removes the percentage — once you are past roughly $250/mo, that trade wins. Podia charges the most at beginner scale and removes the ceiling on what the business can become. Lemon Squeezy removed itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start where your first job lives, run the fee maths on your real price before you commit, and let outgrowing your first platform be the good problem it is supposed to be.
  </p>
